    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  A <span class='learn-keyword'>constitution</span> is the supreme law of a country. It sets out how the country is to be governed, the rights of citizens, and the powers and limits of government. Nigeria has had several constitutions since independence, and understanding their origin, structure, and features helps citizens appreciate the rules that bind the nation together.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Meaning and Origin of the Nigerian Constitution</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>The Nigerian Constitution is the body of fundamental laws that establishes the structure of government, distributes powers among its arms, and guarantees the rights of citizens. Nigeria's constitutional development passed through colonial constitutions (Clifford 1922, Richards 1946, Macpherson 1951, Lyttleton 1954) before independence, and post-independence constitutions in 1960, 1963, 1979, 1989 (unimplemented), 1995 (unimplemented) and the current <strong>1999 Constitution</strong> (as amended), which returned Nigeria to civilian rule.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Structure of the 1999 Constitution</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>The 1999 Constitution is organized into Chapters and Schedules. It begins with general provisions, followed by Fundamental Objectives and Directive Principles of State Policy, Fundamental Human Rights, and the structure of the Executive, Legislature and Judiciary at federal and state levels, plus Local Government provisions.</p>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Chapter</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Chapter I</td><td>General Provisions (supremacy of the constitution, the Federation)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chapter II</td><td>Fundamental Objectives and Directive Principles of State Policy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chapter III</td><td>Citizenship</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chapter IV</td><td>Fundamental Human Rights</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chapter V-VII</td><td>The Legislature, Executive, Judiciary</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chapter VIII</td><td>Federal Capital Territory and General Supplementary Provisions</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Features of the 1999 Constitution</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Key features include: it is a <strong>written</strong> constitution; it is <strong>rigid</strong> (amendment requires special majorities in the National Assembly and State Houses of Assembly); it establishes a <strong>federal</strong> system of government; it provides for <strong>separation of powers</strong> among the three arms of government; it is <strong>supreme</strong>, meaning any law inconsistent with it is void; and it guarantees fundamental human rights.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>dY'!</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners like to test the difference between a rigid and a flexible constitution, and the correct chronological order of Nigeria's constitutions. The 1999 Constitution is Nigeria's current, longest-surviving written constitution.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>dYZ_</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The Nigerian Constitution is the supreme law that has evolved from colonial charters to the 1999 Constitution (as amended). It is written, rigid, federal, and provides for separation of powers and fundamental rights.\n</div>\n  ",
