    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  Chapter II of the 1999 Constitution contains the <span class='learn-keyword'>Fundamental Objectives and Directive Principles of State Policy</span>, a set of ideals meant to guide government in making laws and policies for the wellbeing of citizens, covering political, economic, social, and educational goals.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Meaning</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>These are guiding statements of national goals that the government should pursue, though they are <strong>non-justiciable</strong> (not directly enforceable in court). They express the kind of society Nigeria aspires to build: one that is just, democratic, and prosperous.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Categories of Objectives</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Focus</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Political Objectives</td><td>Democracy, national integration, rule of law, participation in government</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Economic Objectives</td><td>Free enterprise mixed with social justice; control of resources for common good</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Social Objectives</td><td>Equal opportunities, adequate shelter, food, health care, and welfare</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Educational Objectives</td><td>Free and compulsory education at basic levels; eradication of illiteracy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Others</td><td>Foreign policy objectives, environmental objectives, obligation to enforce the Fundamental Objectives</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Importance</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>These principles serve as a moral compass for government, guiding legislation and policy-making even though citizens cannot sue directly to enforce them. They express Nigeria's national aspirations and are often used by courts to interpret other laws in a manner consistent with these ideals.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>dY'!</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse Fundamental Objectives (Chapter II, non-justiciable) with Fundamental Human Rights (Chapter IV, justiciable). This distinction is a favourite exam question.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>dYZ_</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Fundamental Objectives and Directive Principles cover political, economic, social and educational goals meant to guide government policy toward the welfare of citizens, though they cannot be enforced directly in court.\n</div>\n  ",
