    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  <span class='learn-keyword'>Separation of powers</span> is the constitutional principle that divides government functions among three distinct arms — the Executive, the Legislature, and the Judiciary — so that no single arm holds absolute power. This is reinforced by a system of <span class='learn-keyword'>checks and balances</span> that allows each arm to limit the powers of the others.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Meaning and Origin</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>The doctrine was popularized by the French philosopher Baron de Montesquieu, who argued that liberty is best protected when legislative, executive, and judicial powers are held by separate bodies. Nigeria's 1999 Constitution adopts this doctrine: the National Assembly makes laws (legislative power), the President and executive council implement laws (executive power), and the courts interpret laws and settle disputes (judicial power).</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. The Three Arms of Government</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Function</th><th>Example Body</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Legislature</td><td>Makes laws, approves budget, oversight of executive</td><td>National Assembly (Senate & House of Reps)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Executive</td><td>Implements and enforces laws, day-to-day administration</td><td>President, Vice President, Ministers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Judiciary</td><td>Interprets laws, settles disputes, protects rights</td><td>Supreme Court, Court of Appeal, Federal High Court</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Checks and Balances</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Each arm can check the powers of the others: the legislature can override a presidential veto or impeach the President; the executive can veto bills passed by the legislature; the judiciary can declare laws or executive actions unconstitutional through judicial review. This interdependence prevents abuse of power while still allowing government to function.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Separation of powers and checks and balances are related but distinct — separation of powers divides functions, while checks and balances allows each arm to limit the others. Examiners often test this distinction with named examples like veto, impeachment, and judicial review.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Separation of powers divides government into Legislature, Executive, and Judiciary, each with distinct functions, while checks and balances allow the arms to limit one another and prevent abuse of power.\n</div>\n  ",
