    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  Nigeria is home to a rich collection of <span class='learn-keyword'>heritage sites</span> — places of historical, cultural, or natural significance including UNESCO World Heritage Sites, national museums, and national parks. These sites preserve Nigeria's identity and history for present and future generations.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Meaning of Heritage Sites</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>A heritage site is a place recognized for its cultural, historical, archaeological, or natural importance, often protected by law or international convention for preservation and public education. Heritage sites help citizens connect with their history and identity.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Examples of Nigerian Heritage Sites</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Site</th><th>Type / Significance</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Sukur Cultural Landscape (Adamawa)</td><td>Nigeria's first UNESCO World Heritage Site; traditional Hidi (chiefdom) landscape</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove (Osun State)</td><td>UNESCO World Heritage Site; sacred forest dedicated to the goddess Osun</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>National Museum, Lagos and Jos</td><td>Preserve artefacts like the Nok terracotta, Benin Bronzes replicas, and ethnographic items</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Yankari National Park (Bauchi)</td><td>Major wildlife park with the Wikki Warm Spring</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Kainji National Park</td><td>Nigeria's first and largest national park</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nok Culture sites (Kaduna/Plateau)</td><td>Archaeological sites of the ancient Nok civilization famed for terracotta art</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Importance of Heritage Sites</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Heritage sites promote tourism and revenue generation, educate citizens about history and culture, foster national pride and identity, and preserve biodiversity (in the case of national parks). International recognition, such as UNESCO listing, also brings global attention and funding support for preservation.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember Sukur was Nigeria's FIRST UNESCO World Heritage Site (inscribed 1999), while Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove followed later (2005). Exams often ask which came first.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigerian heritage sites include UNESCO sites (Sukur, Osun-Osogbo), national museums, and national parks (Yankari, Kainji). They preserve history, boost tourism, and strengthen national identity.\n</div>\n  ",
