    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  <span class='learn-keyword'>Federalism</span> is a system of government in which power is constitutionally shared between a central (federal) government and constituent units (states). Nigeria practices federalism, and understanding its features, challenges, and importance is central to understanding Nigerian governance.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Meaning and Features of Nigerian Federalism</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Federalism divides governmental powers between two or more levels of government, each with authority over specific matters, as defined by the constitution. Key features of Nigerian federalism include: a written and rigid constitution; a two-chamber (bicameral) legislature; division of powers into Exclusive, Concurrent, and Residual Legislative Lists; an independent judiciary; and revenue allocation among federal, state, and local governments.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Legislative Lists</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>List</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Exclusive List</td><td>Matters only the federal government can legislate on</td><td>Defence, foreign affairs, currency</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Concurrent List</td><td>Matters both federal and state governments can legislate on</td><td>Education, health, electric power</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Residual List</td><td>Matters left to state governments (not explicitly listed)</td><td>Local policing of markets, agriculture at state level</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Importance of Federalism</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Federalism accommodates Nigeria's ethnic, cultural, and religious diversity by allowing states some autonomy; it brings governance closer to the people; it allows for healthy competition and experimentation in policy among states; and it helps prevent excessive concentration of power at the centre.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>4. Challenges of Nigerian Federalism</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Challenges include over-centralization of power and resources at the federal level, revenue allocation disputes among states, agitation for resource control (especially in oil-producing states), state creation demands, and tension between federal character principles and merit-based appointments.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to give at least one example each of items on the Exclusive, Concurrent, and Residual Lists — this is a frequently tested area.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigerian federalism divides power between federal and state governments through Exclusive, Concurrent, and Residual Lists, accommodating diversity, though it faces challenges like over-centralization and resource control disputes.\n</div>\n  ",
