    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  <span class='learn-keyword'>Foreign policy</span> refers to a country's strategy and principles guiding its relations with other nations and international organizations. Since independence, Nigeria has pursued a foreign policy centered on African leadership and unity, earning it the description of an 'Afrocentric' power.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Meaning of Foreign Policy</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Foreign policy is the set of goals, strategies, and actions a government adopts in dealing with other states, international bodies, and global issues, aimed at protecting national interests while contributing to international peace and cooperation.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Principles of Nigerian Foreign Policy</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Africa as the centrepiece</td><td>Prioritizing African unity, development, and interests in foreign relations</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Respect for sovereignty and non-interference</td><td>Respecting the independence and internal affairs of other states</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Peaceful settlement of disputes</td><td>Preferring dialogue and diplomacy over conflict</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Promotion of world peace and international cooperation</td><td>Active participation in the UN and peacekeeping</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Non-alignment</td><td>Avoiding automatic allegiance to global power blocs (historically, Cold War East/West)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Objectives and Achievements</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Key objectives include promoting African unity and integration, protecting Nigerian citizens abroad, attracting foreign investment, and championing decolonization and anti-apartheid causes historically. Achievements include Nigeria's leading role in founding the Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) in 1975, active participation in African Union (AU) affairs, and significant contributions of troops and resources to peacekeeping missions (e.g., in Liberia and Sierra Leone through ECOMOG).</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>4. Challenges</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Challenges include domestic economic and security problems limiting Nigeria's global influence, inconsistency in foreign policy execution across administrations, and the tension between prioritizing African interests and pursuing narrower national interests.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember 'Africa as the centrepiece of Nigerian foreign policy' is a key phrase frequently quoted in exams — know what it means and give examples like ECOWAS and ECOMOG.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigerian foreign policy is guided by principles like African centrality, sovereignty, and peaceful dispute settlement, with achievements including founding ECOWAS and leading peacekeeping efforts in West Africa.\n</div>\n  ",
