    "topic": "Citizenship in a Digital Age",
    "topicCode": "SS2-CHS-15",
    "module": "Nigeria and the World",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  The rise of the internet and digital technology has created new dimensions of citizenship. <span class='learn-keyword'>Digital citizenship</span> involves understanding cyber rights, avoiding cybercrime, and exercising responsible digital behaviour in an increasingly connected world.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Meaning of Digital Citizenship</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Digital citizenship refers to the responsible, ethical, and informed use of technology and the internet by individuals participating in the digital society. It includes understanding one's rights online (cyber rights) as well as responsibilities to behave lawfully and ethically in digital spaces.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Cyber Rights</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Right</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Right to privacy</td><td>Protection of personal data from unauthorized access or misuse</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Freedom of expression online</td><td>Right to share opinions, within legal limits (e.g., not inciting violence)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Access to information</td><td>Right to seek and receive information via digital platforms</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Protection from cyberbullying/harassment</td><td>Right to be free from online abuse and harassment</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Cybercrime</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Cybercrime refers to criminal activities carried out using computers or the internet, including internet fraud (e.g., '419' scams and phishing), hacking, identity theft, cyberbullying, and the spread of malicious software (malware). Nigeria's Cybercrimes (Prohibition, Prevention, etc.) Act 2015 provides the legal framework to combat these offences.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>4. Digital Responsibility</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Digital responsibility involves verifying information before sharing it (combating misinformation/'fake news'), respecting others' privacy and rights online, protecting personal data through strong passwords and safe browsing habits, avoiding cyberbullying, and using digital platforms constructively for learning, civic engagement, and economic opportunity.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know the name of Nigeria's key cybercrime law — the Cybercrimes (Prohibition, Prevention, etc.) Act 2015 — and be able to distinguish cyber rights from digital responsibilities.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Digital citizenship in the internet age involves understanding cyber rights (privacy, expression, protection from abuse), recognizing cybercrime, and practicing responsible, ethical online behaviour.\n</div>\n  ",
