    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  Studying <span class='learn-keyword'>role models</span> in Nigerian citizenship and heritage helps students see how real individuals have exemplified patriotism, public service, cultural preservation, and civic courage — providing practical inspiration beyond theory.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Purpose of Case Studies</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Case studies of notable Nigerians allow students to connect abstract concepts of citizenship (like patriotism, integrity, and civic responsibility) to real lives and achievements, illustrating how individual actions can shape national development and preserve heritage.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Examples of Nigerian Role Models</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Role Model</th><th>Contribution</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Chief Obafemi Awolowo</td><td>Pioneered free primary education in Western Nigeria; nation-building statesman</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Dr. Nnamdi Azikiwe</td><td>Nigeria's first President; independence movement leader and pan-Africanist</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chief Margaret Ekpo</td><td>Women's rights and political activist; pioneering female politician</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti</td><td>Women's rights activist and educator, championed female suffrage in Nigeria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prof. Wole Soyinka</td><td>Nobel laureate; cultural and literary figure who champions human rights and heritage</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Chinua Achebe</td><td>Author who preserved and projected African/Nigerian culture through literature</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Common Themes Among Role Models</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Despite different fields, these role models share themes: courage to challenge injustice, commitment to public service and nation-building, dedication to education and empowerment of others, and pride in and promotion of Nigerian/African culture and heritage.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>4. Applying Lessons Today</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Students are encouraged to identify qualities from these role models — integrity, service, courage, cultural pride — and apply them in their own conduct as students and future citizens, recognizing that positive citizenship can start with individual action at any level of society.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to match a role model's name to their primary contribution — exams often test this directly, so know at least one key achievement for each major figure studied.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigerian role models like Awolowo, Azikiwe, Ransome-Kuti, and Soyinka demonstrate citizenship values in action — service, courage, education, and cultural pride — offering practical lessons for students today.\n</div>\n  ",
