    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  Nigeria's democracy relies on key <span class='learn-keyword'>democratic institutions</span> — including the electoral commission, political parties, and the judiciary — that work together to enable free elections, political competition, and the rule of law.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. The Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC)</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>INEC is the constitutionally established body responsible for organizing, conducting, and supervising elections in Nigeria at the federal and state levels. Its functions include voter registration, election logistics, candidate nomination oversight, vote collation, and declaration of results.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Political Parties</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Political parties are organizations that nominate candidates for elections and compete for political power based on shared ideologies or platforms. They aggregate citizens' interests, offer policy alternatives, and provide a structured pathway for citizens to participate in governance through voting and party membership.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. The Judiciary in Democracy</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>The judiciary, particularly election tribunals and appellate courts, plays a crucial role in resolving electoral disputes, interpreting electoral laws, and ensuring that democratic processes comply with the constitution — acting as a critical check on both INEC and political actors.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Institution</th><th>Key Democratic Function</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>INEC</td><td>Organizes and supervises elections; registers voters and parties</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Political Parties</td><td>Nominate candidates; represent policy platforms; mobilize voters</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Judiciary/Election Tribunals</td><td>Resolve electoral disputes; interpret electoral laws</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>National/State Assemblies</td><td>Make and amend electoral laws; oversight of INEC's budget/operations</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>4. Importance of Strong Democratic Institutions</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Strong, independent democratic institutions ensure credible elections, peaceful transfer of power, accountability of leaders, and citizens' confidence in the political system, all essential for sustaining democracy over time.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know INEC's full name — Independent National Electoral Commission — and its core functions; exams frequently test the acronym and its role distinctly from political parties and the judiciary.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigeria's democracy depends on institutions like INEC (organizing elections), political parties (representing citizens' choices), and the judiciary (resolving disputes) working together to sustain democratic governance.\n</div>\n  ",
