    "module": "Democracy and Elections",
    "term": "Third Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  <span class='learn-keyword'>Electoral malpractices</span> are illegal or unethical actions that undermine the credibility and fairness of elections. Recognizing these practices and their consequences is essential for citizens who wish to protect the integrity of Nigeria's democracy.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Common Forms of Electoral Malpractice</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Malpractice</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Vote buying/selling</td><td>Offering or accepting money/gifts in exchange for votes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ballot box snatching/stuffing</td><td>Forcibly seizing ballot boxes or illegally inserting extra ballots</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Thuggery and violence</td><td>Using intimidation or physical force to disrupt voting or scare voters/officials</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Result manipulation</td><td>Altering collated figures to favour a particular candidate</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Multiple voting/impersonation</td><td>A person voting more than once or voting as someone else</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Under-age voting</td><td>Allowing persons below the legal voting age to vote</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Causes of Electoral Malpractice</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Causes include weak enforcement of electoral laws, desperation for political power/office, poverty (making vote-buying effective), poor security at polling units, and a culture of impunity where perpetrators face few real consequences.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Effects of Electoral Malpractice</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Electoral malpractice produces illegitimate or contested leadership, erodes public trust in democracy, can trigger post-election violence and instability, discourages genuine political competition, and undermines development as unaccountable leaders may prioritize personal over public interest.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>4. Combating Electoral Malpractice</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Solutions include strict enforcement of electoral laws and prosecution of offenders, use of technology (like BVAS) to reduce manipulation, robust security deployment at polling units, civic education to discourage vote-selling, and active citizen and civil society monitoring/reporting of irregularities.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to classify a described scenario as a specific type of malpractice (e.g., 'thugs seized ballot boxes' = ballot box snatching) — WAEC-style questions often test this identification skill.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Electoral malpractices — vote buying, ballot snatching, violence, and result manipulation — undermine democratic credibility, but can be curbed through law enforcement, technology, and citizen vigilance.\n</div>\n  ",
