    "contentHTML": "\n<div class='learn-intro'>\n  <span class='learn-keyword'>Environmental citizenship</span> is the recognition that individuals have rights and responsibilities toward protecting the environment. As Nigeria faces growing environmental challenges, responsible citizen action is essential for sustainable development.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>1. Meaning of Environmental Citizenship</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Environmental citizenship involves individuals understanding their impact on the environment and taking responsible action — through daily habits, advocacy, and civic engagement — to protect natural resources and promote sustainability for present and future generations.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>2. Key Environmental Challenges in Nigeria</h3>\n<div class='learn-table-wrap'>\n  <table class='learn-table'>\n    <thead><tr><th>Challenge</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Deforestation</td><td>Loss of forest cover due to logging, farming, and urban expansion</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Desertification</td><td>Northern Nigeria losing arable land to advancing desert conditions</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Oil pollution</td><td>Oil spills degrading land and water, especially in the Niger Delta</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Flooding</td><td>Poor drainage and climate change increasing destructive flood events</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Waste management</td><td>Inadequate systems for managing household and industrial waste</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Erosion</td><td>Soil erosion, especially gully erosion in South-Eastern Nigeria</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>3. Environmental Rights and Duties</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Chapter II of the constitution directs government to protect and improve the environment. Citizens correspondingly have duties to avoid activities like illegal dumping, bush burning, and pollution, and to actively participate in environmental protection efforts such as tree planting and proper waste disposal.</p>\n\n<h3 class='learn-subheading'>4. Practical Environmental Citizenship Actions</h3>\n<p class='learn-p'>Citizens can practice environmental citizenship by properly disposing of waste and supporting recycling, conserving water and energy, participating in tree-planting and clean-up campaigns, avoiding practices like bush burning and illegal logging, and advocating for stronger environmental protection policies.</p>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to match a specific Nigerian environmental challenge to its typical region — e.g., desertification (North), oil pollution (Niger Delta), gully erosion (South-East) — a commonly tested pairing.\n</div>\n\n<div class='learn-tip-box'>\n  <span class='learn-tip-icon'>🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Environmental citizenship means recognizing responsibility toward the environment amid challenges like deforestation, oil pollution, and erosion, requiring practical individual action alongside government policy.\n</div>\n  ",
